But there is one problem ❌

If I were reviewing this code as a software engineer, I would not keep it this way.

Why?

Notice this:

respond_to_cpu(cpu_status)

respond_to_ram(ram_status)

respond_to_disk(disk_status)

We're responding three separate times.

But our AI already made a decision:

AI Decision

↓

POSSIBLE_ATTACK

So why ignore it?

A Better Design (We'll implement this later)

Instead of:

CPU HIGH

↓

Respond

and

RAM HIGH

↓

Respond

We'll do:

CPU HIGH

RAM HIGH

↓

Decision Engine

↓

POSSIBLE_ATTACK

↓

Response Engine

↓

Take ONE coordinated action

Then the response engine will decide:

Kill suspicious process
Block IP
Isolate endpoint
Send notification
Save logs

This is much closer to how real EDR/XDR platforms operate.
